1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,000
♪ ♪ ♪

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,070
Hi, it's Dessi Zaharieva and I'm here to talk to you about how

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:12,480
the same exercise type might lead to different glucose outcomes.

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:14,130
A little about me:

5
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:18,900
I'm a Certified Exercise Physiologist, Diabetes Educator and Researcher,

6
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:22,500
and have been living with Type One Diabetes myself for over 28 years.

7
00:00:23,835 --> 00:00:27,015
Today we'll chat about why the same activity can

8
00:00:27,015 --> 00:00:29,805
affect your glucose levels differently depending on

9
00:00:29,805 --> 00:00:32,715
how hard you're working or the situation you're in.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
♪ ♪ ♪

11
00:00:37,595 --> 00:00:40,775
How hard you're working or the intensity of an activity

12
00:00:40,955 --> 00:00:43,894
may cause different responses to your glucose levels.

13
00:00:44,495 --> 00:00:45,905
Here are some considerations:

14
00:00:47,139 --> 00:00:50,709
Low to moderate intensity activities like brisk walking

15
00:00:50,739 --> 00:00:53,679
or running may cause your glucose levels to drop.

16
00:00:54,879 --> 00:00:58,419
Mixed intensity activities like soccer or CrossFit

17
00:00:58,750 --> 00:01:01,779
may cause your glucose levels to go up or down,

18
00:01:02,289 --> 00:01:04,660
depending on how much of that activity is high

19
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,660
intensity versus low to moderate intensity.

20
00:01:08,065 --> 00:01:11,575
To learn more about mixed intensity exercise and how to prepare

21
00:01:11,575 --> 00:01:15,565
for it, listen to our episode on mixed intensity exercise.

22
00:01:16,705 --> 00:01:20,755
High intensity activities like explosive sprinting, especially

23
00:01:20,755 --> 00:01:24,265
while fasting may cause your glucose levels to rise.

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
♪ ♪ ♪

25
00:01:29,449 --> 00:01:31,119
Let's discuss the science behind it.

26
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:35,199
During low intensity activities like walking,

27
00:01:35,499 --> 00:01:37,659
fat is the body's preferred fuel source.

28
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:42,490
As the intensity of exercise increases, like jogging or

29
00:01:42,490 --> 00:01:46,329
running, the body relies on fat and carbs for energy.

30
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:50,050
Your liver will release glucose into the bloodstream

31
00:01:50,169 --> 00:01:52,869
to try and match what the muscles are using,

32
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:55,359
but it typically can't keep up with the

33
00:01:55,359 --> 00:01:57,729
glucose demand of those working muscles.

34
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:00,460
This is why you often need to eat extra

35
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:03,789
carbs before or during a run to avoid lows.

36
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:09,190
Another option is to turn on a Tidepool Loop activity preset

37
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:13,239
an hour before exercise starts to let the app know that

38
00:02:13,239 --> 00:02:16,900
you need less insulin than usual when you begin exercise.

39
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:21,364
For high intensity exercise like explosive sprinting or power

40
00:02:21,364 --> 00:02:25,624
lifting your body typically relies more on carbs for fuel,

41
00:02:26,045 --> 00:02:28,894
which means your muscles break down stored glycogen,

42
00:02:28,984 --> 00:02:32,644
your body's form of stored carbs, for quick energy,

43
00:02:32,945 --> 00:02:36,094
while your liver releases more glucose into the bloodstream.

44
00:02:36,695 --> 00:02:39,484
However, your muscles can't keep up with the amount of

45
00:02:39,484 --> 00:02:43,445
glucose, and this often leads to a rise in glucose levels.

46
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:48,235
In the Tidepool Loop app, the presets for exercise

47
00:02:48,235 --> 00:02:51,385
section includes a tool to help you understand how the

48
00:02:51,385 --> 00:02:54,985
intensity of an activity might affect your glucose levels.

49
00:02:58,585 --> 00:03:00,325
Why context matters:

50
00:03:01,045 --> 00:03:03,865
The situation you're in can also change how

51
00:03:03,865 --> 00:03:05,845
an activity affects your glucose levels.

52
00:03:06,685 --> 00:03:08,365
Let's use swimming as an example.

53
00:03:08,695 --> 00:03:12,085
During swim practice, when you're swimming laps, you might

54
00:03:12,085 --> 00:03:15,925
see similar changes or drops in your glucose levels each week.

55
00:03:16,734 --> 00:03:20,904
But on a competition day with adrenaline pumping friends and family

56
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:25,374
cheering you on, and the pressure to win glucose levels may rise.

57
00:03:25,825 --> 00:03:29,635
More specifically during competition stress hormones like

58
00:03:29,635 --> 00:03:33,595
adrenaline and cortisol are released signaling to the liver

59
00:03:33,595 --> 00:03:37,230
to release glucose to the bloodstream to provide quick energy.

60
00:03:38,019 --> 00:03:41,200
In individuals with Type One Diabetes, this response

61
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:44,229
is not balanced by an appropriate increase in insulin,

62
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,890
therefore, often leading to a rise in glucose levels.

63
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:52,240
Additionally, adrenaline can reduce muscle glucose

64
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:55,960
uptake by making the cells less sensitive to insulin.

65
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:59,920
This combination of increased glucose production by the

66
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,180
liver and reduced uptake by the muscles can result in

67
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:08,710
hyperglycemia or high glucose levels during intense exercise.

68
00:04:09,369 --> 00:04:11,739
So what do you do in these situations?

69
00:04:12,399 --> 00:04:16,659
Remember, you might need different strategies depending on your situation.

70
00:04:16,959 --> 00:04:20,919
Regular practices may require less insulin, or you may

71
00:04:20,919 --> 00:04:24,340
need extra carbs on hand to reduce the risk of lows.

72
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:27,940
But on game day, monitor your glucose levels and be

73
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:31,510
aware that your body may not need less insulin and

74
00:04:31,510 --> 00:04:34,840
more carbs like you've often done on practice days.

75
00:04:38,934 --> 00:04:41,155
I'd love to share a personal example.

76
00:04:41,695 --> 00:04:46,135
I had been training TaeKwonDo for over 15 years and qualified

77
00:04:46,135 --> 00:04:49,104
to represent Team Canada at the World Championships.

78
00:04:49,945 --> 00:04:54,744
I had spent countless hours in practice and attending local competitions.

79
00:04:55,315 --> 00:04:58,104
I was familiar with how my glucose levels responded

80
00:04:58,104 --> 00:05:01,375
during practice or even smaller competitions.

81
00:05:01,794 --> 00:05:04,674
Usually with practice, my glucose levels stayed

82
00:05:04,674 --> 00:05:07,825
pretty stable or dropped from many hours of activity.

83
00:05:08,515 --> 00:05:12,325
But when I competed at my very first TaeKwonDo World Championships

84
00:05:12,325 --> 00:05:17,245
for Team Canada, everything changed. Before the competition

85
00:05:17,455 --> 00:05:20,935
my glucose levels were steady around 180 milligrams

86
00:05:20,935 --> 00:05:25,075
per deciliter, and I felt pretty good, albeit nervous.

87
00:05:25,885 --> 00:05:28,765
After my first match, which lasted less than 10

88
00:05:28,765 --> 00:05:32,065
minutes, my glucose levels rose to the four hundreds.

89
00:05:33,025 --> 00:05:35,335
The adrenaline impact was so real.

90
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
♪ ♪ ♪

91
00:05:40,365 --> 00:05:44,535
Before we wrap up, let's take 15 minutes this week to notice how

92
00:05:44,535 --> 00:05:48,945
exercise intensity and context might affect your glucose levels.

93
00:05:49,455 --> 00:05:51,555
Here are a few things to explore:

94
00:05:52,485 --> 00:05:54,705
Do your glucose levels respond differently

95
00:05:54,705 --> 00:05:57,300
during practices versus games or races?

96
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:01,695
How does increasing or decreasing the intensity

97
00:06:01,695 --> 00:06:04,395
of an activity affect your glucose levels?

98
00:06:04,815 --> 00:06:07,065
What happens to your glucose levels when you

99
00:06:07,065 --> 00:06:09,885
do walking versus running versus sprinting?

100
00:06:10,455 --> 00:06:13,785
If you play competitive sports, try visualizing

101
00:06:13,785 --> 00:06:16,485
the event to prepare yourself for game day.

102
00:06:17,205 --> 00:06:19,875
Imagine you're stepping into the competition ring

103
00:06:20,055 --> 00:06:22,935
or on the field, whatever that may be for you.

104
00:06:23,655 --> 00:06:28,770
Repeat this at the end of every practice for five to 10 minutes if you can.

105
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:33,270
This might help you handle stressful situations better because

106
00:06:33,270 --> 00:06:37,500
you've already been there in your mind and prepared for this moment.

107
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:42,210
Understanding these differences can help you plan better for

108
00:06:42,210 --> 00:06:45,960
physical activity and manage your glucose levels more effectively.

109
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:53,000
♪ ♪ ♪
